{\looseness=-1 \textbf{Sparse routing, multi-horizon, and task generalization.}\label{sec:topk}
Top-2 sparse routing adds $4.3$\,ms ($+10\%$) while retaining $63\%$ of the dense gain; scaling $K{=}3{\to}20$ changes MSE by ${<}5\%$. RR-MoA wins \textbf{24/24} across $H{\in}\{96{-}720\}$ and generalizes to imputation ($7/8$ datasets; Appendix~\ref{app:topk},~\ref{app:imputation}).}

\vspace{-0.5em}
\section{Conclusion}
\vspace{-0.3em}

We identified \emph{normalization-induced routing collapse} as the root cause of MoE adapter failure on time series foundation models. Eight causal controls---including opposite entropy trajectories on identical data (Figure~\ref{fig:causal_contrast})---isolate the mechanism, and a mutual-information decomposition predicts where it applies ($\rho{=}{-}0.88$, $n{=}9$). Because the diagnosis pinpoints the cause, the fix principle is clear: \textbf{RR-MoA} routes on raw, pre-normalization input, achieving \textbf{54/54 wins} and a Frozen Paradox where frozen adapters outperform full fine-tuning by $12$--$79\%$. The diagnosis is further validated by its actionability: restoring raw-signal access to experts matches or beats DLinear on $107/123$ cells across six backbones.

\textbf{Practitioner guideline.} Before deploying MoE adapters on TSFMs with instance normalization, compute $R(\mathcal{D})$: in our experiments, values above $0.5$ predict raw routing helps; below $0.2$, it does not (validated on 9 LTSF datasets). \textbf{Limitations:} Time-MoE~\citep{shi2024timemoe} is untested; Proposition~\ref{prop:frozen} covers linear backbones only (Figure~\ref{fig:trajectory} confirms the mechanism in full Transformers); $R(\mathcal{D})$ uses first moments and may miss higher-order routing signals (Solar outlier).

