/Users/hungpq2412/neuralips26/main.tex:55:Time series foundation models (TSFMs) like MOMENT and Moirai provide powerful pretrained representations, but adapting them to a downstream task requires attaching a lightweight adapter head that maps hidden states to predictions. Standard TSFM benchmarks use a single static head---one adapter topology for every input window. A natural improvement is a mixture-of-expert (MoE) adapter, where a router selects the best expert head for each window. However, on TSFMs with instance normalization, this \textbf{catastrophically fails}: the router learns to send \emph{every} window to the \emph{same} expert---routing entropy collapses to $0.000{\pm}0.000$, and the remaining experts are never used. We identify \textbf{normalization-induced routing collapse}: instance normalization strips the per-window mean and variance that the router needs to distinguish input regimes, leaving it with homogenized hidden states that all look alike. Eight converging causal controls isolate the mechanism---most strikingly, opposite entropy trajectories on identical data (Timer-XL rises $1.49{\to}1.60$ while MOMENT collapses $0.60{\to}0.00$)---and all five standard MoE-rescue families (load balancing, z-loss, entropy regularization, ReLU routing, expert-choice) across eleven configurations fail ($2.7{\times}$ worse; 720 runs). A mutual-information decomposition formalizes the mechanism and yields a signal-ratio predictor ($\rho{=}{-}0.88$, $p{<}0.002$, $n{=}9$) that identifies where collapse occurs, including boundary cases where it does \emph{not} (Traffic, $R{=}0.14$). Because the diagnosis pinpoints the cause, the fix principle is clear: \textbf{Raw-Routed Mixture of Adapters (RR-MoA)} routes on raw, pre-normalization input, achieving \textbf{54/54 wins} ($p{<}0.001$) on the primary backbone and generalizing across four additional backbones (two negative controls without instance normalization confirm specificity). A predicted consequence---the \textbf{Frozen Paradox}---is confirmed: frozen adapters beat full fine-tuning by $12$--$79\%$, because unfreezing triggers co-adaptation collapse. An extension restoring raw-signal access to experts matches or beats DLinear on 6/6 datasets (Appendix).
